---
title: Classic Restaurant Obauer Chocolate Mousse
description: A light, airy, and traditional Austrian restaurant-style chocolate mousse using stiffly whipped egg whites alongside soft-whipped cream for a featherweight, cloud-like texture and clean dark chocolate intensity.
servings: 6
prep time: 25 minutes
chill time: 4 hours
difficulty: moderate
cuisine: Austrian
tags:
  - desserts
  - chocolate
  - mousse
  - austrian
  - classic
  - vegetarian
---

= Melt the Chocolate & Butter

Set a #heatproof glass or metal bowl{} over a #saucepan{} with 2–3 cm of gently simmering water (bain-marie), ensuring the water does not touch the base of the bowl.

Add @dark chocolate{200%g}(65%–70% cocoa, finely chopped) and @unsalted butter{30%g}(cubed). Stir occasionally with a #spatula{} until completely melted, glossy, and smooth.

Remove the bowl from the heat and stir in optional @dark rum{1%tbsp}(or strong espresso, optional for aroma).

Let the chocolate mixture cool to lukewarm (around 35°C to 40°C). If it is too warm, it will scramble the egg yolks; if too cold, it will seize.

= Whip the Heavy Cream

Pour thoroughly chilled @heavy whipping cream{250%ml}(Schlagobers, at least 35% milk fat) into a #cold bowl{}.

Whip with a #hand mixer{} or stand mixer until soft peaks form (the cream holds its shape lightly but the tips curl over). Do not overwhip into stiff cream, or folding will become difficult and grainy. Place the whipped cream into the refrigerator until ready to fold.

= Beat the Egg Yolks

> Egg Temperature Tip: Eggs separate most cleanly when cold, but egg whites whip to greater volume and yolks blend better at room temperature. Separate them cold, then let them sit on the counter for ~{20%minutes} before whipping.

Separate @large eggs{4}(room temperature) strictly into yolks and whites, and divide @granulated sugar{50%g} into two equal 25 g portions.

In a #medium bowl{}, combine the 4 egg yolks with the first 25 g portion of @granulated sugar{}. Whisk vigorously with a #whisk{} or electric beaters for ~{3-4%minutes} until the mixture turns pale yellow, thick, and falls back in ribbons.

Slowly pour the lukewarm melted chocolate mixture into the yolk mixture, whisking briskly until fully emulsified and uniform.

= Whip the Egg Whites to Stiff Peaks

> Grease-Free Tip: Ensure your egg white bowl and beaters have zero traces of yolk or fat; even a droplet of grease will prevent the whites from achieving maximum volume.

In a clean, completely grease-free #metal or glass bowl{}, add the 4 egg whites and @fine sea salt{1%pinch}. Beat on medium speed until frothy and white.

Gradually stream in the remaining 25 g of @granulated sugar{} while increasing the mixer speed to high. Continue whipping until stiff, glossy peaks form that stand upright without bending over when the beaters are lifted.

= Fold Together

Add roughly one-third of the chilled whipped @heavy whipping cream{} into the chocolate-yolk base. Stir gently with a #silicone spatula{} until incorporated to loosen the dense chocolate mixture.

Add half of the whipped egg whites. Use slow, sweeping, circular folding motions—cut down the center, sweep along the bottom of the bowl, and lift up over the top while rotating the bowl.

Fold in the remaining whipped @heavy whipping cream{}, followed by the remaining half of the whipped egg whites. Stop folding the second there are no visible streaks of white foam left so you preserve the delicate micro-bubbles.

= Portion & Chill

Gently spoon or pipe the mousse into individual #dessert cups{} or transfer into a large #porcelain serving bowl{}.

Cover lightly with plastic wrap and refrigerate for at least ~{4-6%hours}, or preferably overnight, to allow the cocoa butter and proteins to set properly.

> Traditional Presentation: Warm an oval soup spoon in hot water, wipe it dry, and scrape the surface of the set mousse toward you in a rolling motion to form a restaurant-style quenelle. Serve alongside lightly stewed sour cherries (Weichseln) or fresh raspberries.
